{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to check the\ncoverage setup under {cite}`requirements.test-coverage.setup.validation` and\nhelp establish or repair it with `habeas setup` before bootstrapping requirement\nevidence. It MUST direct agents to report conflicting configuration for review\nrather than overwrite project-specific nextest settings.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.test-coverage.setup.bootstrap` states only what the skill text must tell agents to do. It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or named interface for the implementation. The clauses are:\n\n- \"MUST direct agents to check the coverage setup under `requirements.test-coverage.setup.validation`\".\n- \"help establish or repair it with `habeas setup` before bootstrapping requirement evidence\".\n- \"report conflicting configuration for review rather than overwrite project-specific nextest settings\".\n\nEach clause is about the skill's instruction content. A skill that gave the same directions with different wording, section placement or step order would satisfy all three. None of them binds how the document is built. The citation of `requirements.test-coverage.setup.validation` is context, not a structural obligation. That requirement's own checks belong to the validation code, not to this skill.\n\nI read the supplied root, `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`. Workflow step 1, in `section[Bootstrap%20requirements]/section[Workflow]/paragraph@2cfd1398048b`, covers the behavior:\n\n- It tells agents to check coverage readiness with `habeas status` before bootstrapping evidence. It notes that the validation test also checks the saved `.config/nextest.toml` collector and default-profile rules.\n- It tells agents to use `habeas setup` to establish or repair the integration.\n- It tells agents to report conflicting nextest settings for review and to preserve project-specific settings.\n\nThat is behavioral coverage of the text, and no implementation edge is needed for a structural obligation.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement sets three directives for the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill. The only test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_checks_and_repairs_coverage_setup_and_preserves_conflicts]`, loads the bundled skill through `bundled_skill`. That helper joins SKILL.md with its reference files and collapses whitespace. The test then asserts that the text contains each required phrase.\n\n1. **Check the coverage setup under `requirements.test-coverage.setup.validation` before bootstrapping evidence.** The test asserts \"Before bootstrapping evidence, check coverage readiness with `habeas status`\". That phrase carries both the check and its ordering ahead of bootstrapping. The test also asserts \"saved `.config/nextest.toml` collector and default-profile rules\", which ties the check to what the validation requirement inspects.\n2. **Help establish or repair the setup with `habeas setup`.** The test asserts \"Use `habeas setup` to establish or repair\".\n3. **Report conflicting configuration for review rather than overwrite project-specific nextest settings.** The test asserts \"Report conflicting nextest settings for review\" and \"preserve project-specific settings\".\n\nI read workflow step 1 in `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`. It contains these sentences, so the assertions are consistent with it. Removing or changing any directive would fail the test.\n\nThe obligation is guidance text in a skill, so a phrase-presence assertion is the right way to observe it. No other behavioral obligation or boundary needs a test. The test carries the `{test}` marker for the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
